{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The proposal requires selecting the first grounded extension ordered by a polynomial-time computable lexicographic priority. But enumerating ALL grounded extensions to then pick the first is itself exponential in general — there can be exponentially many complete extensions of an argumentation framework (a classic counting result). The 'polynomial-time computable priority' only orders extensions that have already been generated; it does not bound the number of extensions. Thus polynomial cost is not actually preserved, violating the problem's required guarantee — and the target itself concedes 'polynomial cost only holds if the number of extensions is polynomially bounded,' which is not generally true.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"The target states pruning is a function of 'committed roots' (committed warrants), but computing the committed set IS the fixed point the semantics is trying to define. Making pruning depend on committed roots creates a circularity: you need the committed set to prune, but you need the pruned graph to compute the committed set. The target never specifies how this circularity is resolved (e.g., a simultaneous fixed-point operator over the pruned graph and the committed set). Without such a specification the operator is not well-defined, so determinism of the fixed point — a required guarantee — is not actually established.","counterexample":null}]}